     "text": [
      "While **Thomas Edison** is the name most people associate with the invention of the light bulb, he did not actually invent it from scratch. Instead, he **perfected it and made it commercially practical.** \n",
      "\n",
      "The invention of the light bulb was a gradual process involving many inventors over several decades. Here is the breakdown of how it happened:\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. The Early Pioneer: Humphry Davy (1802)\n",
      "Nearly 80 years before Edison’s patent, English scientist **Humphry Davy** invented the first electric light. He connected a battery to carbon rods, creating a glowing arc of light. This was known as the **\"Electric Arc Lamp.\"** However, it was too bright for home use and burned out far too quickly.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. The First Vacuum Bulb: Warren de la Rue (1840)\n",
      "British scientist **Warren de la Rue** passed an electric current through a platinum filament enclosed in a vacuum tube. The vacuum prevented oxygen from reacting with the metal, allowing it to last longer. However, platinum was too expensive, making this bulb impractical for the public.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3. The Chief Rival: Joseph Swan (1878)\n",
      "English physicist **Joseph Swan** was Edison’s biggest rival. In 1878, Swan developed a working light bulb using carbonized paper filaments in a vacuum. He even began installing them in homes in England. \n",
      "* **The conflict:** Swan’s bulbs didn't last very long because his vacuum pumps weren't efficient enough, and his filaments were too thick, requiring too much electricity.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 4. The Perfector: Thomas Edison (1879)\n",
      "Thomas Edison and his team of researchers at Menlo Park, New Jersey, analyzed all previous designs. Edison realized the key to a practical light bulb was:\n",
      "1. A **high vacuum** inside the bulb.\n",
      "2. A **very thin filament** with high electrical resistance.\n",
      "\n",
      "After testing thousands of materials (including cardboard, fishing line, and even beard hair), Edison’s team discovered that a **carbonized bamboo filament** could burn for over **1,200 hours**. \n",
      "\n",
      "In **October 1879**, Edison filed a patent for a practical electric lamp, and this is widely considered the birth of the modern light bulb.\n",
      "\n",
      "### The Edison-Swan Merger\n",
      "Because Joseph Swan had patented a similar bulb in Great Britain before Edison, Edison sued Swan for patent infringement. However, Swan’s patent held up. Instead of fighting, the two inventors joined forces and founded the **Ediswan** company in Great Britain, which dominated the European market.\n",
      "\n",
      "### Summary\n",
      "* **Humphry Davy** invented the first electric light (1802).\n",
      "* **Joseph Swan** invented the first practical carbon-filament light bulb (1878).\n",
      "* **Thomas Edison** improved the design, made it long-lasting, and—most importantly—created the entire electrical grid system (generators, cables, meters) to power it, making it accessible to the world.\n"
     ]
    }
